(Link: http://www.righto.com/2026/10/ML10-switched-capacitor-filter.html) Tj
T*
(Sometimes it's easier to identify an IC with a microscope. While sorting a box of old ICs,) Tj
T*
(CuriousMarc came across some Harris ICs labeled "F1-10-5", a mysterious part number that didn't) Tj
T*
(show up in any databooks. Since unidentifiable ICs are useless, he gave me one to analyze.) Tj
T*
(Conveniently, it was in a ceramic package, so I could open it up with a quick tap from a) Tj
T*
(chisel. Under the microscope, the chip's most striking feature was a grid of square capacitors.) Tj
T*
(With all those capacitors, I guessed that it was a switched-capacitor filter. The die provided) Tj
T*
(another clue: the part number HF-10. With this information, we quickly found that the chip was) Tj
T*
(Harris's version of the standard MF10 switched-capacitor filter chip. 1 The Harris integrated) Tj
T*
(circuit, labeled F1-10-5 \(or maybe FI-10-5\), with a 1985 date code. Photo courtesy of) Tj
T*
(CuriousMarc. Switched-capacitor filters were a popular way to implement analog filters in the) Tj
T*
(1980s. Rapidly switching capacitors in and out of a circuit enabled the construction of) Tj
T*
(single-chip filters that were easy to use and performed well. The MF10, introduced by National) Tj
T*
(Semiconductor in 1981, provides two flexible filters on a chip; each filter acts as a low...) Tj
